#Day12- Post 2: Hands-On Docker Stack Deployment on an Amazon Linux Cluster

In this post, I’ll walk you through deploying a Docker Stack on a Swarm cluster running on Amazon Linux servers. We’ll set up a cluster with 1 master (manager) and 2 worker nodes and deploy two web services—web1 and web2—ensuring these run only on the manager node.

Step 1: Initialize the Swarm on the Master Node 
Log in as the root user on your Amazon Linux master node, then run:


docker swarm init --advertise-addr <manager-ip>


Replace <manager-ip> with your manager node’s IP address. This command initializes your Swarm and designates the node as the manager.

Step 2: Join the Worker Nodes to the Swarm 
On each of your two worker nodes (also as root), join the swarm using the token generated during the `swarm init`:


docker swarm join --token <worker-token> <manager-ip>:2377


You can get the `<worker-token>` by running `docker swarm join-token worker` on your manager node. Verify the nodes with:

 docker node ls 

Step 3: Create Your Docker compose File 
Create a file named `compose.yml` with the following content. This file defines two services, web1 and web2.

---
version: "3"
services:
 web1:
 container_name: cont1
 image: nginx
 deploy:
 replicas:3
 ports:
 - "8081:80"
 
 web2:
 image: nginx
 deploy:
 replicas:3
 ports:
 - "8082:80"



In this example, we use the nginx image for simplicity. Replace it with your desired image if needed.

Step 4: Deploy the Stack
Deploy the stack on your manager node by executing:


docker stack deploy my_stack –compose-file=compose.yml


This command deploys the stack using your YAML file. You can replace `my_stack` with a stack name of your choice.

 Step 5: Verify Your Deployment 
Check your services’ status:


docker service ls
docker service ps my_stack_web1
docker service ps my_stack_web2


This verification confirms that web1 and web2 are deployed and running exclusively on the manager node, as specified by the placement constraints.



Final Thoughts: 
This two-part approach helps in understanding the underlying concepts before diving into a practical demo. With Docker Stack, you simplify complex, multi-service deployments, and tying it with Docker Swarm makes your applications scalable and resilient.
